The new version of DROD, 2.0, is free, as previous versions have been.
But it comes as a demo of a game called Journey to Rooted Hold. You
can use the demo to play hundreds of holds (dungeons), the vast
majority of which are free. But if you want to finish Journey to
Rooted Hold, you have to pay, and there are also other things from
their web site that you have to pay for. And their site is set up so
that you find out about the things that cost money -- and also about
the JtRH demo -- before you learn about anything that's free.

In short, the people at Caravel Games have taken an open-source game
and kept it free, while adding features you have to pay for. And
they've done a pretty good job of maximizing the chances that people
will pay for them. That's smart web marketing, in my book.

The only weird part is that they try to make people pay for King
Dugan's Dungeon, which is also available for free. I don't understand
that.

